// A MODULE-QUALIFIED type name (`dep.Point`, `data.Data`) must resolve in every
// position a type is written — design 150 says so outright: "a qualifier works
// in EVERY position a name appears".
//
// It does not, in three of them, and they are the same three the design-194
// unit-4 prelude gate had to be wired into by hand: a struct FIELD, an enum
// PAYLOAD, and a `type` alias right-hand side. Those annotations are stored RAW
// and read straight off the AST, so they never reach `_resolve_type` — the one
// place that walks a module qualifier — and the dotted spelling survives into
// type comparison, where `dep.Point` and `Point` are two different types:
//
//     error: field `p` expects type `dep.Point` but got `Point`
//
// `_canonical_type_name` returns a dotted name unchanged on purpose ("for
// `_resolve_type`'s module-walk branch to handle"), and for these three slots
// that branch never runs. A fourth face is a constructor's generic ARGUMENT:
// `Vector<data.Data>()` binds a local whose element type keeps the dot.
//
// This file uses `std.data` rather than a helper module because the shape is
// identical for both and the std spelling is the one design 194 unit 4 makes
// people reach for: the gate's own hint offers `import std.data` +
// `data.Data` as one of three ways to satisfy it, and in a struct field that
// advice does not work.
//
// XFAIL: DF-194a a module-qualified type name does not resolve in a struct field, an enum payload or a `type` alias — the three annotation slots stored raw, which never reach `_resolve_type`
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// 2

import std.data

struct Holder {
    d: data.Data
}

extension Holder: NoCopy {}

func main() {
    var d = data.Data()
    d.push(65)
    d.push(66)
    let h = Holder(d: move d)
    print(h.d.len())
}
